was his brother George, who was fighting with the 51st New York. Walt followed the 51st in the

01:12:28.900 --> 01:12:29.480
newspapers.

01:12:30.120 --> 01:12:35.440
through its battles at Roanoke Island, Kelly's Ford, Second Bull Run, Antietam,

01:12:36.040 --> 01:12:40.980
and then at Fredericksburg, Virginia, where on December 13, 1862,

01:12:41.520 --> 01:12:47.300
the Union's commanding general had ordered 14 futile attacks against fortified rebel positions.

01:12:49.100 --> 01:12:53.140
When George's name appeared in the casualty lists in the New York newspapers

01:12:53.140 --> 01:12:59.980
just before Christmas of 1862, Walt headed to the front to find his name.

01:13:00.030 --> 01:13:13.480
brother. Whitman comes to Fredericksburg not knowing what he's going to find and walks by

01:13:13.860 --> 01:13:23.650
the mansion that has been turned into the hospital and what he recalls seeing is a pile

01:13:23.650 --> 01:13:29.860
of severed limbs, amputated limbs, arms and legs.

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:40.930
of the soldiers and this war becomes for him a war on the body as well as a war between

01:13:40.930 --> 01:13:51.500
the states. George Whitman was alive and well, but Walt remained in Fredericksburg for more

01:13:51.500 --> 01:13:58.160
than a week, drawing nearer the war each day. In his small green notebook, Whitman began

01:13:58.160 --> 01:13:59.460
recording the sights.

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:06.980
sounds and the smells of the camp, and the soldier language of the front. He walked the

01:14:06.980 --> 01:14:13.160
charred and denuded landscape of the now still battlefield, watched the silent burial teams at

01:14:13.160 --> 01:14:20.970
work, pulled back the army issue blankets to look on faces of the dead, and visited with the men

01:14:20.970 --> 01:14:29.680
who would soon enough be dead. I do not see that I do much good to these wounded and dying.

01:14:30.670 --> 01:14:32.030
but I cannot leave them.

01:14:34.820 --> 01:14:38.780
Once in a while, some youngster holds on to me convulsively,

01:14:38.780 --> 01:14:42.100
and I do what I can for him.

01:14:42.930 --> 01:14:47.590
At any rate, stop with him and sit near him for hours if he wishes it.

01:14:50.450 --> 01:14:52.950
Doctors in the field hospital noticed Whitman

01:14:52.950 --> 01:14:57.580
and asked him to tend to a trainload of casualties headed north to the capital.

01:14:59.060 --> 01:14:59.980
When a few of the wounded...

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:05.900
on that train asked the favor of his company at the hospitals in Washington, Walt could not refuse.

01:15:10.560 --> 01:15:16.640
The hospitals Whitman entered were a primitive business. Surgeons sharpened knives on their

01:15:16.640 --> 01:15:23.510
boot soles. We knew nothing about antiseptics, wrote one doctor, and therefore used none.

01:15:26.740 --> 01:15:28.780
Infection cut through those wards.

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:33.420
and green cases had to watch as their tissue was eaten away.

01:15:34.510 --> 01:15:38.890
The smell was so bad they were often isolated and left to die alone.

01:15:42.570 --> 01:15:48.770
You hear groans or other sounds of unendurable suffering from two or three cots,

01:15:50.030 --> 01:15:52.110
but in the main, there's quiet.

01:15:54.020 --> 01:15:59.380
Most of these sick or hurt are entirely without friend or acquaintances here.

01:16:01.880 --> 01:16:08.370
In one of the hospitals, I find Thomas Halley, shot through the lungs, inevitably dying.

01:16:10.550 --> 01:16:12.990
Poor fellow is like a frightened animal.

01:16:16.140 --> 01:16:19.040
What was a man to do, Walt would later write.

01:16:19.810 --> 01:16:24.950
There were thousands, tens of thousands, hundreds of thousands needing me.

01:16:26.670 --> 01:16:29.050
He found a job as a copyist.

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:36.810
And he would work until noon, then he would go to the wards from noon until 4.

01:16:38.080 --> 01:16:44.070
At 4 he would go back to the Connors' house, he would take a bath, he would refresh himself,

01:16:44.270 --> 01:16:51.480
probably take a nap, have a supper, and then at 6 he would return to the ward and stay from 6 to 9.

01:16:52.590 --> 01:16:57.620
But if a particular soldier wanted his company, he would stay the night at the hospital

01:16:57.620 --> 01:16:59.970
and go directly from the ward to work.

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:07.040
the next morning in the spring of 1863 Whitman made his rounds from his small

01:17:07.040 --> 01:17:11.720
apartment to his government office building to the 40 hospitals in and

01:17:11.720 --> 01:17:17.820
around Washington roaming through a nervous city uniformed soldiers peopled

01:17:17.820 --> 01:17:22.120
the streets and bumped down in the Capitol or in the park next to the

01:17:22.120 --> 01:17:26.800
Washington Monument whose construction had stalled at a squat 152 feet

01:17:26.800 --> 01:17:29.900
completion was no sure

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:35.980
thing news of general lee's victory over the union forces at chancellorsville echoed through

01:17:35.980 --> 01:17:44.280
the capital city the rebels were at the gates for all anyone knew the squad of the guard patrolling

01:17:44.280 --> 01:17:53.180
about examining passes a sentry at the door a cavalry man on horseback with naked saber over

01:17:53.180 --> 01:17:59.750
his shoulder like a statue at the corner a great white

01:18:00.090 --> 01:18:05.750
Aladdin's palace with an unfinished dome, reported to be cracking.

01:18:07.560 --> 01:18:11.660
A goddess of liberty, meanwhile, standing in the mud waiting.

01:18:14.360 --> 01:18:20.800
The light falls, falls, touches the cold white of the great public edifices.

01:18:21.990 --> 01:18:28.520
Touches with a kind of death glaze here and there, the windows of Washington.

01:18:31.560 --> 01:18:38.950
This awful scene, Whitman had come to believe, was beyond a poet's healing, certainly beyond his healing.

01:18:40.380 --> 01:18:43.660
Walt had no plans to make new additions of Leaves of Grass.

01:18:44.540 --> 01:18:47.400
He had dedicated himself to his duty in the hospitals.

01:18:48.260 --> 01:18:49.900
That was all he had to give.

01:18:51.220 --> 01:18:55.920
What hope he still had for the continuation of America's grand democratic experiment,

01:18:55.920 --> 01:18:58.450
he grafted on to a new savior.

01:19:00.860 --> 01:19:03.580
I see the president almost every day, he wrote.

01:19:04.880 --> 01:19:08.360
We have got to where we exchange bows and very cordial ones.

01:19:09.360 --> 01:19:14.100
I never see the man without feeling that he is one to become personally attached to.

01:19:16.560 --> 01:19:23.990
Lincoln embodied everything that the I, the first person of Lee's Degrass, was meant to embody.

01:19:25.500 --> 01:19:29.970
He was the average, ordinary, everyday American, and yet...

01:19:30.250 --> 01:19:31.950
He was gifted with such eloquence.

01:19:32.090 --> 01:19:33.590
He had a kind of poetic side to him.

01:19:37.340 --> 01:19:40.700
Apparently Walt used to wait in front of the White House

01:19:40.700 --> 01:19:45.760
just for a glimpse of his redeemer president, as he called him.

01:19:46.590 --> 01:19:51.780
I remember a quote that Lincoln was so ugly, he was beautiful.

01:19:56.880 --> 01:19:59.980
Walt may have recognized something of himself in the...

01:20:00.030 --> 01:20:07.960
stooped shoulders and sad eyes of the besieged and unpopular president. In 1863, Abraham Lincoln

01:20:07.960 --> 01:20:14.660
was under attack for instituting the first federal income tax and a military draft, and for signing

01:20:14.660 --> 01:20:20.230
the controversial Emancipation Proclamation, freeing the slaves in any state and rebellion.

01:20:21.670 --> 01:20:27.690
Whitman backed him on all of it. Above all, Walt honored Lincoln's vow to save the Union,

01:20:28.350 --> 01:20:29.970
even as the terrible cost of...

01:20:40.870 --> 01:20:47.290
The war, Whitman wrote, seems to me like a great slaughterhouse

01:20:47.290 --> 01:20:50.120
and the men mutually butchering each other.

01:20:52.520 --> 01:20:55.940
When I am present at the most appalling scenes,

01:20:56.380 --> 01:20:59.470
deaths, operations, sickening wounds.

01:21:00.350 --> 01:21:27.020
full of maggots i do not give out or budge but often hours afterwards i feel sick and actually tremble yesterday was the worst many with bad and bloody wounds inevitably long neglected the sight of some cases brought tears to my eyes

01:21:31.280 --> 01:21:34.600
I had the luck yesterday, however, to do some good.

01:21:37.080 --> 01:21:43.440
He would get dressed up and wash his beard,

01:21:43.440 --> 01:21:47.210
and all the young soldiers would call him old man,

01:21:47.210 --> 01:21:50.800
even though he was only 40-some years old at the time,

01:21:52.020 --> 01:21:55.440
because he really did look, as one soldier said,

01:21:55.580 --> 01:21:58.080
like Santa Claus coming through the wards,

01:21:58.080 --> 01:21:59.840
and he was carrying his little bag.

01:22:00.090 --> 01:22:05.130
He would give them candy and he would give them treats and he would take down their requests

01:22:05.130 --> 01:22:07.770
for small things that he would go and get.

01:22:09.520 --> 01:22:16.400
His bag contained tobacco aplenty and like a good mother, no child ever got more than

01:22:16.400 --> 01:22:17.140
the other child.

01:22:19.220 --> 01:22:24.760
He would have notepaper, he would have jelly, he would have pens, he would have pickles,

01:22:25.140 --> 01:22:29.740
he would have biscuits, any treat.

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:31.660
that a soldier could have imagined.

01:22:35.000 --> 01:22:38.430
There was a moment when he gave 15 cents

01:22:38.430 --> 01:22:40.650
to one of these boys and the boy said,

01:22:41.390 --> 01:22:43.930
I'm gonna buy milk from the milk lady

01:22:43.930 --> 01:22:47.110
and then burst into tears.

01:23:06.330 --> 01:23:13.530
The intensity of that sense of encountering the strangers again

01:23:13.530 --> 01:23:18.570
and feeling tremendous affection for them,

01:23:19.610 --> 01:23:21.150
demonstrating that affection.

01:23:22.130 --> 01:23:24.210
Many of the soldiers, as they were dying,

01:23:24.630 --> 01:23:26.320
the last kiss they would have,

01:23:26.440 --> 01:23:29.730
the last moment of affection they would have would be from this.

01:23:30.380 --> 01:23:40.400
bearded poet who had taken time to stop with them there were these again moments of what he had

01:23:40.400 --> 01:23:46.750
learned in new york to be those moments of urban affection that in the hospital became national

01:23:46.750 --> 01:23:53.250
affection all of these soldiers from all over the country southern soldiers as well as northern

01:23:53.250 --> 01:23:59.900
soldiers there really was a sense in those hospital wards of whitman encountering the

01:24:00.030 --> 01:24:08.020
country, the entire nation, in a way that he never would in any other form, in any other setting.

01:24:09.500 --> 01:24:15.130
They were all there, and he would absorb it all, show affection for them all.

01:24:19.470 --> 01:24:28.880
I've spent a long time with Oscar F. Wilbur Company G, 154th New York, low with chronic diarrhea.

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:31.220
And a bad wound also.

01:24:33.740 --> 01:24:37.620
He talked of death and said he did not fear it.

01:24:39.980 --> 01:24:43.700
He behaved very manly and affectionate.

01:24:45.880 --> 01:24:47.020
The kiss I gave him.

01:24:47.540 --> 01:24:50.770
As I was leaving, he returned fourfold.

01:24:53.980 --> 01:24:55.580
He died a few days after.

01:25:10.680 --> 01:25:18.940
Whitman's efforts in the hospitals, 600 visits, he figured, to more than 100,000 patients, left him near collapse.

01:25:20.130 --> 01:25:27.320
He suffered from insomnia, night sweats, night terrors, headaches, sore throat, and buzzing in the ears.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:34.920
Alone with his ailments, in a Spartan third-floor walk-up ten blocks from the White House,

01:25:35.950 --> 01:25:40.450
Walt Whitman found strength enough to extend one final and solemn service

01:25:40.450 --> 01:25:43.090
to the soldiers he'd come to know so well.

01:25:44.520 --> 01:25:49.000
He opened his notebooks and began sketching his memorial to them all.

01:25:49.820 --> 01:25:52.860
In a new book of poetry he called Drum Taps.

01:25:55.120 --> 01:25:59.970
From the stump of the arm, the amputated hand, I am...

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:10.860
undo the clotted lint remove the slough wash off the matter and blood back on his pillow the

01:26:10.920 --> 01:26:27.220
soldier bends with curved neck and side falling head his eyes are closed his face is pale he

01:26:27.220 --> 01:26:28.940
who dares not look on the bloody stump.

01:26:30.120 --> 01:26:31.900
and has not yet looked on.

01:27:00.510 --> 01:27:10.030
I am faithful. I do not give out the fractured thigh, the knee, the wound in the abdomen.

01:27:10.350 --> 01:27:13.370
These and more I dress with impassive hand.

01:27:14.100 --> 01:27:17.450
Yet deep in my breast a fire, a burning flame.

01:27:18.140 --> 01:27:25.620
Thus in silence, in dreams projections, returning, resuming, I thread my way through the hospitals.

01:27:25.980 --> 01:27:29.560
The hurt and the wounded I pacify with soothing hand.

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:32.420
I sit by the restless all the dark night.

01:27:36.100 --> 01:27:41.820
Some are so young, some suffer so much.

01:27:57.940 --> 01:27:59.780
Walt Whitman completed the manuscript.

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:03.300
for drum taps around the time of Lincoln's second inauguration.

01:28:04.300 --> 01:28:07.360
A month later, Lee surrendered his army at Appomattox,

01:28:07.780 --> 01:28:09.810
and the bloody fighting was over.

01:28:11.300 --> 01:28:13.600
Whitman's hero president had saved the Union,

01:28:14.290 --> 01:28:16.370
and the poet was convinced Abraham Lincoln

01:28:16.370 --> 01:28:18.110
could repair the national breach.

01:28:20.020 --> 01:28:22.460
Five days after the surrender, on Good Friday,

01:28:23.370 --> 01:28:25.690
Walt was back home in Brooklyn with his family.

01:28:26.540 --> 01:28:29.760
George was finally home too, after four years at war.

01:28:30.510 --> 01:28:38.000
jeff was prospering drum taps was ready for the printer an early bloom of daffodils hyacinths

01:28:38.000 --> 01:28:45.450
and tulips scented portland avenue and there at his mother's house whitman received news

01:28:45.450 --> 01:28:55.390
of the last sad casualty of the long war southerners as well as northerners were

01:28:55.390 --> 01:28:59.980
We're stunned and we're, on some level, grieved.

01:29:00.990 --> 01:29:03.910
by the assassination of Abraham Lincoln.

01:29:07.580 --> 01:29:11.200
So Lincoln becomes, in death, in a sense,

01:29:12.280 --> 01:29:14.580
a greater version of what he had been in life.

01:29:19.470 --> 01:29:23.320
Coffin that passes through lanes and streets,

01:29:24.550 --> 01:29:25.570
through day and night,

01:29:26.220 --> 01:29:28.340
with the great cloud darkening the land.

01:29:30.730 --> 01:29:35.420
with the pomp of the in-loop flags, with the cities draped in black,

01:29:37.060 --> 01:29:41.860
with the show of the states themselves, as of crepe-veiled women standing,

01:29:44.140 --> 01:29:48.450
with processions long and winding, and the flambeaus of the night,

01:29:49.060 --> 01:29:55.030
with the countless torches lit, with the silent sea of faces, and the unbared heads.

01:30:00.000 --> 01:30:06.070
Walt Whitman, who, above all, had been searching for unity, comradeship, togetherness, feels

01:30:06.070 --> 01:30:13.740
that in the death of Abraham Lincoln, we finally have that kind of unity that America had lacked

01:30:13.740 --> 01:30:22.910
before that, the unity that is finally achieved and that Whitman's early poetry could try

01:30:22.910 --> 01:30:24.350
to achieve but never could.

01:30:27.270 --> 01:30:29.870
Then there is a cement to the whole.

01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:38.380
people, subtle, more underlying than anything in written constitution or courts or armies,

01:30:39.600 --> 01:30:48.110
namely the cement of a death identified thoroughly with that people at its head and for its sake.

01:30:50.550 --> 01:30:58.800
Strange, is it not, that battles, martyrs, agonies, blood, even assassination,

01:31:00.640 --> 01:31:05.180
should so condense, perhaps only really lastingly

01:31:05.180 --> 01:31:08.040
condense a nationality.

01:31:08.880 --> 01:31:12.540
It's only going to be after the Civil War

01:31:12.540 --> 01:31:16.920
that he is going to begin to reconceive

01:31:16.920 --> 01:31:21.210
and reimagine Leaves of Grass so radically

01:31:21.210 --> 01:31:25.550
that after the Civil War, he would actually say

01:31:25.550 --> 01:31:28.370
at one point that the Civil War is the very heart

01:31:28.370 --> 01:31:29.970
and the center of Leaves of Grass.

01:31:30.220 --> 01:31:46.680
around which the book works in the 1867 edition he actually sews in i think of it as an act of

01:31:46.680 --> 01:31:59.420
suturing drum taps the civil war poem into the back of leaves of grass and in that act

01:32:00.380 --> 01:32:05.790
He has made the decision that leaves of grass is large enough,

01:32:06.450 --> 01:32:13.200
absorptive enough, broad enough to absorb this national disaster.

01:32:15.710 --> 01:32:19.630
What new life now for America can possibly grow out of it?

01:32:40.620 --> 01:32:44.880
The war completed leaves of grass and put a period on an era.

01:32:46.420 --> 01:32:49.520
Walt Whitman spent many of the following 25 years

01:32:49.520 --> 01:32:52.980
confined to a small house he bought in Camden, New Jersey,

01:32:53.780 --> 01:32:57.760
while the country the poet had so lovingly absorbed faded from view.

01:32:59.130 --> 01:32:59.970
Post-war America...

01:33:00.060 --> 01:33:26.720
I'll see you next time.

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:35.570
surrounded his heart one growth eroded a rib leaving him in excruciating pain he

01:33:36.170 --> 01:33:43.890
had an enlarged prostate a fatty liver and a gallstone at the end of the 1880s he

01:33:43.890 --> 01:33:48.290
was wheelchair-bound and really found one side of his body completely paralyzed

01:33:48.290 --> 01:33:55.600
so this was a man on all fronts you know intellectual political personal saw

01:33:55.600 --> 01:33:57.580
some debilitating changes

01:34:01.050 --> 01:34:19.660
nearing curious thou dim uncertain specter is thou life or death drink weakness blindness

01:34:20.650 --> 01:34:29.590
more paralysis and heavier our placid skies and sun will stir the waters yet

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:45.840
or happily cut me short for good or leave me here as now dull parrot-like and old with cracked voice

01:34:45.840 --> 01:34:56.260
harping greaching you have poems that are complaining about old age and about

01:34:56.790 --> 01:34:59.890
prickettiness and you know he even writes about his

01:35:00.030 --> 01:35:04.650
condition you learn he's got diarrhea you know but to him that's important he's

01:35:04.650 --> 01:35:12.280
putting his body on paper but there's something else too there's there's an

01:35:12.280 --> 01:35:16.700
idea that there's something beyond the body it's as if he knows he's got to

01:35:16.700 --> 01:35:21.920
leave that shell behind and yet there's something to look forward to also

01:35:25.760 --> 01:35:29.380
whispering something again

01:35:30.380 --> 01:35:46.310
unseen where late this heated day thou interest at my window thou laving tempering all cool

01:35:46.560 --> 01:35:50.080
Freshing, gently vitalizing.

01:36:00.570 --> 01:36:01.150
It melted.

01:36:02.490 --> 01:36:02.890
Lord.

01:36:03.130 --> 01:36:03.710
With sweat.

01:36:06.780 --> 01:36:07.740
Not nestling.

01:36:08.890 --> 01:36:11.290
Folding close and firm.

01:36:12.250 --> 01:36:13.130
Let's off.

01:36:15.700 --> 01:36:17.400
opinion better than talk

01:36:18.940 --> 01:36:19.200
Bull.

01:36:24.890 --> 01:36:26.350
What's striking to me...

01:36:27.220 --> 01:36:28.900
is how he lets down his guard.

01:36:30.120 --> 01:36:32.400
And how he honestly expresses him.

01:36:32.780 --> 01:36:33.880
self as a

01:36:34.660 --> 01:36:35.240
a thick

01:36:35.880 --> 01:36:37.240
Old man grateful

01:36:38.340 --> 01:36:38.720
for

01:36:39.180 --> 01:36:40.080
A moment's breeze.

01:36:40.970 --> 01:36:42.770
which may or may not be indicative

01:36:43.430 --> 01:36:45.110
of something out there in the universe.

01:36:47.560 --> 01:36:50.640
So sweet thy primitive taste.

01:36:51.500 --> 01:36:52.620
to breathe within.

01:36:54.180 --> 01:36:57.860
thy soothing fingers on my face and hands.

01:37:00.310 --> 01:37:06.190
Thou messenger, magical strange bringer to body and spirit of me.

01:37:07.030 --> 01:37:08.510
Distance is bogged.

01:37:09.180 --> 01:37:13.260
Occult medicines penetrating me from head to foot.

01:37:15.640 --> 01:37:17.060
I feel the sky.

01:37:18.780 --> 01:37:20.340
The prairie's vast.

01:37:20.540 --> 01:37:21.520
I feel...

01:37:21.940 --> 01:37:23.740
The mighty northern lakes.

01:37:25.020 --> 01:37:26.460
I feel the ocean...

01:37:26.650 --> 01:37:27.650
and the forest.

01:37:28.980 --> 01:37:29.660
Somehow.

01:37:30.000 --> 01:37:34.000
I feel the globe itself swift swimming in space.

01:37:37.320 --> 01:37:37.920
Well...

01:37:37.930 --> 01:37:39.830
Blown from lips so loved

01:37:40.100 --> 01:37:40.940
No, gone.

01:37:42.890 --> 01:37:44.550
happily from endless store.

01:37:44.840 --> 01:37:45.500
God sent.

01:37:47.620 --> 01:37:50.620
For thou art spiritual, godly.

01:37:52.940 --> 01:37:55.820
Minister to speak to me here and now.

01:37:56.330 --> 01:37:59.770
what word has never told and cannot tell.

01:38:00.660 --> 01:38:01.320
has now

01:38:01.720 --> 01:38:02.540
No soul?

01:38:05.400 --> 01:38:06.620
Can I not know?

01:38:09.270 --> 01:38:10.090
Identify thee.

01:38:27.700 --> 01:38:29.920
Walt Whitman died in Camden, New Jersey.

01:38:30.410 --> 01:38:31.370
in 1892.

01:38:33.000 --> 01:38:34.020
his life's work.

01:38:34.410 --> 01:38:35.490
leaves of grass.

01:38:35.820 --> 01:38:37.500
which he had tended obsessively.

01:38:37.770 --> 01:38:39.170
for 35 years.

